## Figures from code

Figures generated from Python, R, or Julia code are **automatically** treated as figures.

### Python Example

You can include the following (this generates a plot output and echos the original commands)

::: {.cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.plot([1, 23, 2, 4])
plt.show()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![A simple line plot](figures_files/figure-pdf/fig-line-plot-output-1.pdf){#fig-line-plot fig-alt='Line graph showing values increasing and decreasing across four observations.' fig-pos='H'}
:::
:::


::: {.callout}

Adding  `#| echo: false` at the start of the figure will make sure to not echo the code and only display the figure:

::: {.cell execution_count=2}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![A simple line plot](figures_files/figure-pdf/fig-line-plot-noecho-output-1.pdf){#fig-line-plot-noecho fig-alt='A simplified plot with the `#| echo: false` to not show the code'}
:::
:::


:::



